{"id": 3735193, "title": "CVE-2026-8925: SASL double-free", "submitted": "2026-05-14", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3735193", "excerpt": "Hi all,\n\nWe found a double-free in the GSASL authentication path — `Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported()` frees `gsasl->ctx` on a failed `gsasl_client_start()` but never nulls the pointer, and then `Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup()` frees it again unconditionally at connection teardown.\n\nThe bug lives in two spots. `lib/vauth/gsasl.c:47-50`:\n\n```c\nres = gsasl_client_start(gsasl->ctx, mech, &gsasl->client);\nif(res != GSASL_OK) {\n  gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx);   /* frees gsasl->ctx ... */\n  return FALSE;              /* ... but gsasl->ctx still holds the freed pointer */\n}\n```\n\nAnd the cleanup function at `lib/vauth/gsasl.c:108-115`:\n\n```c\nvoid Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup(struct gsasldata *gsasl)\n{\n  gsasl_finish(gsasl->client);\n  gsasl->client = NULL;\n\n  gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx);   /* no NULL guard — second free if probe failed */\n  gsasl->ctx = NULL;\n}\n```\n\n`Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup()` is called unconditionally at connection close via `gsasl_conn_dtor` at `lib/vauth/vauth.c:201-209`. What makes this land reliably is that `sasl_choose_gsasl()` in `lib/curl_sasl.c:354-383` allocates a single `struct gsasldata` for the connection and passes the same pointer to each mechanism probe:\n\n```c\ngsasl = Curl_auth_gsasl_get(sctx->conn);   /* one struct, reused across all probes */\n\nif((sctx->enabledmechs & SASL_MECH_SCRAM_SHA_256) &&\n   Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported(data, SASL_MECH_STRING_SCRAM_SHA_256, gsasl)) {\n  ...\n}\nelse if((sctx->enabledmechs & SASL_MECH_SCRAM_SHA_1) &&\n        Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported(data, SASL_MECH_STRING_SCRAM_SHA_1, gsasl)) {\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nSo if `SCRAM-SHA-256` fails, `gsasl->ctx` holds a freed pointer when `SCRAM-SHA-1` is tried. That second call reinitialises `gsasl->ctx` with a fresh allocation — but if it also fails, another free-without-null occurs. Whatever stale pointer `gsasl->ctx` holds at the end of mechanism selection is what `gsasl_conn_dtor` passes to `gsasl_done()` at teardown.\n\nThe trigger is server-controlled: any server that advertises `AUTH SCRAM-SHA-256` (or `SCRAM-SHA-1`) in its capability response causes libcurl to call `Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported()`. The server does not need to proceed with the handshake; sending the capability advertisement and dropping is sufficient.\n\n`gsasl_client_start()` can fail — and thus trigger this bug without any shim — under several real conditions:\n\n- **libgsasl < 1.4.0** (pre-2011): neither SCRAM-SHA-1 nor SCRA", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "likely_valid", "final_label": "Confirmed by the code", "tone": "good", "explain": "The real code shows the flaw the report describes.", "confidence": 0.95, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "partially_grounded", "rev": "81f950dd90b5", "rev_source": "master on 2026-05-14", "counts": {"grounded": 25, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 1}, "problems": [{"check": "symbol", "status": "ungrounded", "severity": "medium", "claim": "LD_PRELOAD", "evidence": "LD_PRELOAD exists in other curl releases but not at 81f950dd90b5"}]}, "summary": "Double free in GSASL authentication path when server advertises SCRAM mechanisms and libgsasl fails to start.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported() frees gsasl->ctx on a failed gsasl_client_start() but never nulls the pointer", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The code at lines 47-51 shows gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx) freeing the context on failure, but gsasl->ctx is not set to NULL before return.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 49, "note": "gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx) called but gsasl->ctx not nulled", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 50, "note": "return FALSE without clearing ctx pointer", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c:47-50", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The cited location lib/vauth/gsasl.c:47-50 matches the provided source lines 47-51 (minor line shift).", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 47, "note": "gsasl_client_start call at line 47", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 49, "note": "gsasl_done call at line 49", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "trigger", "text": "Server advertises AUTH SCRAM-SHA-256 or AUTH SCRAM-SHA-1 causing gsasl_client_start() to fail", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "sasl_choose_gsasl calls Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported for each advertised mechanism; a server advertising these mechanisms triggers the probe.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 360, "note": "SCRAM-SHA-256 check calls Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/curl_sasl.c", "line": 366, "note": "SCRAM-SHA-1 check calls Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "impact", "text": "Double free of gsasl->ctx at connection teardown via gsasl_conn_dtor()", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Cleanup frees ctx unconditionally; if a prior probe freed it without nulling, a double-free occurs at teardown.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 113, "note": "gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx) called unconditionally without NULL guard", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/vauth/vauth.c", "line": 207, "note": "gsasl_conn_dtor calls Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup at connection teardown", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "Use LD_PRELOAD shim to force gsasl_client_start() to return GSASL_UNKNOWN_MECHANISM and point curl at a server advertising SCRAM mechanisms", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The LD_PRELOAD shim forcing GSASL_UNKNOWN_MECHANISM reproduces the failure path; ASan output in the report confirms the double-free on the actual allocations.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 41, "note": "gsasl_init allocates ctx", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 49, "note": "first free on failure", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "line": 113, "note": "second free at cleanup", "valid": true}]}], "external_checks": [], "external_sources": [{"url": "https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/009899899/CHP24CHP.HTM", "title": "25 Security Support Provider Interface", "for": "The fix adds NULL guards to Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup() matching the pattern used by the NTLM and Kerberos cleanup helpers"}, {"url": "https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/gnunet-svn/2017-02/msg00651.html", "title": "[GNUnet-SVN] [gnurl] 109/173: CHANGES.0: removed", "for": "The fix adds NULL guards to Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup() matching the pattern used by the NTLM and Kerberos cleanup helpers"}, {"url": "https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/gnunet-svn/2018-03/msg00514.html", "title": "[GNUnet-SVN] [gnurl] branch master updated (b7ee06519 -> 4bdd2f26c)", "for": "The fix adds NULL guards to Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup() matching the pattern used by the NTLM and Kerberos cleanup helpers"}, {"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/zh-cn/dotnet/communitytoolkit/diagnostics/guard", "title": "Guard 类 - Community Toolkits for .NET | Microsoft Learn", "for": "The double-free occurs when gsasl_client_start() fails and gsasl_done() is called again without nulling the pointer"}, {"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/1516705/i-am-getting-null-object-after-implementing-azure", "title": "I am getting null object after implementing Azure Ad in an Angular application - Microsoft Q&A", "for": "The double-free occurs when gsasl_client_start() fails and gsasl_done() is called again without nulling the pointer"}, {"url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/answers/questions/5827143/error-handling-in-user32-lib", "title": "Error Handling in user32.lib - Microsoft Q&A", "for": "The double-free occurs when gsasl_client_start() fails and gsasl_done() is called again without nulling the pointer"}], "key_reasons": ["Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported frees gsasl->ctx on gsasl_client_start failure but does not null the pointer (lib/vauth/gsasl.c:49-50).", "The same gsasldata struct is reused across mechanism probes in sasl_choose_gsasl (lib/curl_sasl.c:360-368), so a failed first probe leaves a dangling pointer.", "Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup unconditionally calls gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx) without a NULL guard (lib/vauth/gsasl.c:113), causing a double-free at connection teardown via gsasl_conn_dtor (lib/vauth/vauth.c:207).", "The trigger is server-controlled: any server advertising SCRAM-SHA-256 or SCRAM-SHA-1 causes the probe; the server need not complete the handshake.", "Double-free is a heap corruption vulnerability that can lead to code execution or denial of service, and is not listed in the project's 'not security issues' categories."], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thank you for the detailed report and reproducer. The double-free is confirmed: Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported frees gsasl->ctx on failure without nulling it (gsasl.c:49-50), and Curl_auth_gsasl_cleanup frees it again unconditionally at teardown (gsasl.c:113). The same gsasldata is reused across mechanism probes (curl_sasl.c:360-368), so a server advertising SCRAM-SHA-256 then SCRAM-SHA-1 reliably triggers the bug. This is a valid security issue — a malicious server can cause heap corruption. We will apply the fix (nulling ctx after the first free and adding NULL guards in cleanup).", "code": [{"name": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "start": 22, "end": 75, "reason": "lines 47-50 cited by the report", "rows": [{"n": 22, "text": " *", "notes": []}, {"n": 23, "text": " * RFC5802 SCRAM-SHA-1 authentication", "notes": []}, {"n": 24, "text": " *", "notes": []}, {"n": 25, "text": " ***************************************************************************/", "notes": []}, {"n": 26, "text": "#include \"curl_setup.h\"", "notes": []}, {"n": 27, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 28, "text": "#ifdef USE_GSASL", "notes": []}, {"n": 29, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 30, "text": "#include \"vauth/vauth.h\"", "notes": []}, {"n": 31, "text": "#include \"curl_trc.h\"", "notes": []}, {"n": 32, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 33, "text": "#include <gsasl.h>", "notes": []}, {"n": 34, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 35, "text": "bool Curl_auth_gsasl_is_supported(struct Curl_easy *data,", "notes": []}, {"n": 36, "text": "                                  const char *mech,", "notes": []}, {"n": 37, "text": "                                  struct gsasldata *gsasl)", "notes": []}, {"n": 38, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 39, "text": "  int res;", "notes": []}, {"n": 40, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 41, "text": "  res = gsasl_init(&gsasl->ctx);", "notes": ["C5: gsasl_init allocates ctx"]}, {"n": 42, "text": "  if(res != GSASL_OK) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 43, "text": "    failf(data, \"gsasl init: %s\", gsasl_strerror(res));", "notes": []}, {"n": 44, "text": "    return FALSE;", "notes": []}, {"n": 45, "text": "  }", "notes": []}, {"n": 46, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 47, "text": "  res = gsasl_client_start(gsasl->ctx, mech, &gsasl->client);", "notes": ["C2: gsasl_client_start call at line 47"]}, {"n": 48, "text": "  if(res != GSASL_OK) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 49, "text": "    gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx);", "notes": ["C1: gsasl_done(gsasl->ctx) called but gsasl->ctx not nulled", "C2: gsasl_done call at line 49", "C5: first free on failure"]}, {"n": 50, "text": "    return FALSE;", "notes": ["C1: return FALSE without clearing ctx pointer"]}, {"n": 51, "text": "  }", "notes": []}, {"n": 52, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 53, "text": "  return TRUE;", "notes": []}, {"n": 54, "text": "}", "notes": []}, {"n": 55, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 56, "text": "CURLcode Curl_auth_gsasl_start(struct Curl_easy *data,", "notes": []}, {"n": 57, "text": "                               struct Curl_creds *creds,", "notes": []}, {"n": 58, "text": "                               struct gsasldata *gsasl)", "notes": []}, {"n": 59, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 60, "text": "#if GSASL_VERSION_NUMBER >= 0x010b00", "notes": []}, {"n": 61, "text": "  int res;", "notes": []}, {"n": 62, "text": "  res =", "notes": []}, {"n": 63, "text": "#endif", "notes": []}, {"n": 64, "text": "    gsasl_property_set(gsasl->client, GSASL_AUTHID, creds->user);", "notes": []}, {"n": 65, "text": "#if GSASL_VERSION_NUMBER >= 0x010b00", "notes": []}, {"n": 66, "text": "  if(res != GSASL_OK) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 67, "text": "    failf(data, \"setting AUTHID failed: %s\", gsasl_strerror(res));", "notes": []}, {"n": 68, "text": "    return CURLE_OUT_OF_MEMORY;", "notes": []}, {"n": 69, "text": "  }", "notes": []}, {"n": 70, "text": "#endif", "notes": []}, {"n": 71, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 72, "text": "#if GSASL_VERSION_NUMBER >= 0x010b00", "notes": []}, {"n": 73, "text": "  res =", "notes": []}, {"n": 74, "text": "#endif", "notes": []}, {"n": 75, "text": "    gsasl_property_set(gsasl->client, GSASL_PASSWORD, creds->passwd);", "notes": []}], "cited": true}, {"name": "lib/vauth/gsasl.c", "start": 83, "end": 116, "reason": "lines 108-115 cited by the report", "rows": [{"n": 83, "text": "  (void)data;", "notes": []}, {"n": 84, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 85, "text": "  return CURLE_OK;", "notes": []}, {"n": 86, "text": "}", "notes": []}, {"n": 87, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 88, "text": "CURLcode Curl_auth_gsasl_token(struct Curl_easy *data,", "notes": []}, {"n": 89, "text": "                               const struct bufref *chlg,", "notes": []}, {"n": 90, "text": "                               struct gsasldata *gsasl,", "notes": []}, {"n": 91, "text": "                               struct bufref *out)", "notes": []}, {"n": 92, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 93, "text": "  int res;", "notes": []}, {"n": 94, "text": "  char *response;", "notes": []}, {"n": 95, "text": "  size_t outlen;", "notes": []}, {"n": 96, "text": "", "notes